      "overall_rating": 0,
      "review_title": "",
      "review_description": "The one thing I learnt from this experience was that if the salesman wants to sell you a 1.5Kw \"system\" then you would expect to have an array of panels that adds up to 1.5Kw ( 8 X 190W= 1.52Kw)BUT you should also check that the AC output of the inverter is at least 1.5Kw as well.This AC output is usually a give away with the model of the inverter xxxxx15000 = 1.5Kwor xxxxx2000 = 2Kw. This Maximum output rating can also be found in the clean energy council website, this is what energex will reference when they do up your contract for you to sell back to grid;http://www.solaraccreditation.com.au/acccec/approvedproducts/inverters/currentinverters.html.What I am basically trying to say is that I went with these guys because they were a very good price compared to others but later found out that they were cheaper because they were cutting corners when it come to the actual numbers. I wanted to buy a 1.5Kw system but what I got was 8 x 185W panels = 1.48Kw (not 1.5Kw) and an inverter that had a maximum AC OUTPUT rating of 1.2Kw ( not 1.5Kw) so their parts were cheaper to buy  and they were able to on sell it cheaper - under cutting everyone else.In summary, I don't have any problem with the \"system\" that I got (it works well) BUT the question must be asked - did I actually get a 1.5 Kw \"system\" ?????? some how I don't think so.........",
